640625, -0.0005326271057128906, 0.005611419677734375, -0.023101806640625, 0.0093994140625, 0.0196685791015625, 0.034942626953125, -0.00399017333984375, -0.0032711029052734375, -0.0127105712890625, 0.01904296875, 0.01202392578125, -0.0107574462890625, 0.036376953125, -0.0283203125, 0.0203857421875, 0.01947021484375, -0.0031414031982421875, 0.0209503173828125, 0.002117156982421875, 0.03741455078125, -0.019744873046875, 0.040283203125, 0.0032405853271484375, 0.033935546875, -0.009002685546875, -0.0031642913818359375, 0.01059722900390625, -0.0036754608154296875, 0.00606536865234375, 0.004302978515625, -0.01309967041015625, 0.020050048828125, 0.05670166015625, -0.0107421875, -0.0151824951171875, -0.0318603515625, 0.013885498046875, -0.0128936767578125, 1.8835067749023438e-05, 0.045562744140625, 0.0259246826171875, 0.0019311904907226562, -0.0263519287109375, -0.0081634521484375, -0.006381988525390625, -0.00499725341796875, -0.0247344970703125, 0.017669677734375, -0.032012939453125, -0.0192413330078125, -0.01056671142578125, -0.00963592529296875, 0.0183258056640625, 0.0103912353515625, -0.00293731689453125, -0.01448822021484375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Large-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, most existing pipelines focus on optimizing datasets for individual tasks in isolation, which limits the ability to leverage shared knowledge across related generative tasks. The central challenge is twofold: first, how to curate high-quality, task-specific datasets; and second, how to organize diverse forms of supervision in a way that reflects the dependencies among different generative capabilities.\n\nTo address these challenges, we introduce a capability-driven data infrastructure\u2014a framework that systematically constructs and organizes data according to the progression of generative skills. In this context, a \"capability\" refers to a specific generative task, such as text-to-image (T2I) synthesis, image editing, or associating images with external knowledge. Our infrastructure combines two key components: (1) capability-specific supervision construction, which builds datasets tailored to each capability, and (2) capability-aligned curriculum scheduling, which sequences data exposure to models based on the logical order in which capabilities are acquired.\n\nOur approach centers on three specialized yet interoperable data engines, each responsible for a distinct type of supervision:\n1. The text-image grounding engine constructs datasets that pair textual descriptions with corresponding images, supporting T2I generation. For example, it collects and filters image-caption pairs from public web sources, applying automated quality checks and human review to ensure relevance and accuracy.\n2. The inter-image transformation engine assembles editing pairs, such as original and modified images with associated transformation instructions, enabling models to learn image editing tasks. This engine leverages both synthetic edits and curated real-world edits, ensuring diversity and consistency.\n3. The image-knowledge association engine links images to structured knowledge entities (e.g., objects, locations, or concepts), facilitating tasks that require external knowledge grounding. This is achieved by mapping images to knowledge bases using automated entity recognition and manual validation.\n\nThese engines are designed to produce complementary and relational supervision: each engine\u2019s output not only supports its primary task but also enriches the others by sharing aligned concepts and annotations. For instance, an image-caption pair from the grounding engine can be linked to knowledge entities, or used as a base for generating editing pairs.\n\nTo ensure consistency and alignment across tasks, we employ caption experts\u2014automated captioning models fine-tuned with human feedback\u2014to harmonize textual descriptions and editing instructions. These experts standardize language across datasets, making it easier for models to transfer knowledge between T2I and editing tasks.\n\nThe curriculum learning strategy organizes data presentation in stages that reflect the dependency order of capability acquisition: models first learn basic T2I grounding, then progress to editing and knowledge association. At each stage, we adjust task composition, visual-concept diversity, data quality, and image resolution. Evaluation is integrated into the loop: targeted retrieval and expert review identify gaps in model performance, prompting resampling or augmentation of underrepresented data.\n\nAt scale, our infrastructure curates a 440M-image T2I corpus, 120M editing pairs, and over 27M image-entity pairs. Data sources include publicly available web datasets, open image repositories, and knowledge bases, with quality control enforced through automated filtering, human annotation, and bias mitigation protocols. We exclude sensitive or private content and monitor for demographic and content biases.\n\nWe train multimodal diffusion models from scratch at two scales (3B and 6B parameters) using this infrastructure. For evaluation, we conduct quantitative benchmarking on CPI-Bench, measuring metrics such as FID, CLIP score, and editing accuracy, and compare against state-of-the-art baselines. Qualitative evaluation includes expert review of generated images across diverse T2I and editing scenarios. Our models achieve competitive or superior performance on CPI-Bench, with FID improvements of 5\u201310% over prior methods and strong human preference scores for image quality and edit fidelity. Qualitative results demonstrate broad visual coverage, versatile rendering, and effective transfer between generative tasks, as illustrated in Figures 2\u20134."}}